    "id": "train-1738",
    "question": "Steve has decided to become a baker. On Mondays, Wednesdays and Fridays, he bakes apple pies. On Tuesdays and Thursdays, he bakes cherry pies. If he bakes 12 pies per day, how many more apple pies does he bake than cherry pies in one week?",
    "gold": "12",
    "answer": "In one week, he bakes 3 * 12 = <<3*12=36>>36 apple pies.\nIn one week, he bakes 2 * 12 = <<2*12=24>>24 cherry pies.\nHe bakes 36 - 24 = <<36-24=12>>12 more apple pies.\n#### 12"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many more apple pies Steve bakes than cherry pies in one week, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total number of apple pies baked in one week.\n2. Calculate the total number of cherry pies baked in one week.\n3. Find the difference between the number of apple pies and cherry pies.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of apple pies baked in one week. Steve bakes apple pies on Mondays, Wednesdays, and Fridays. There are 7 days in a week, so he bakes apple pies on 3 out of these 7 days. Since he bakes 12 apple pies per day, the total number of apple pies baked in one week is:\n\\[ 3 \\times 12 = 36 \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the total number of cherry pies baked in one week. Steve bakes cherry pies on Tuesdays and Thursdays. There are 2 days in a week when he bakes cherry pies, so the total number of cherry pies baked in one week is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\]\n\nNow, we find the difference between the number of apple pies and cherry pies baked in one week:\n\\[ 36 - 24 = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, Steve bakes \\(\\boxed{12}\\) more apple pies than cherry pies in one week.",
